<problem>
<!-- <title>Indefinite integral &#x2D; 1/x</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to="10" exclude="0" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="true" />
<selectFromSequence name="b" from="1" to="10" />


<function name="f" variables="t"><math simplify="numbersPreserveOrder">$a[1] /($b +$a[2] *t)</math></function>

<math name="denom" simplify="numbers">$b +$a[2] *t</math>
<function name="fpart" variables="t">1/($b +$a[2] *t)</function>
<math name="logf">ln(abs($b +$a[2] *t))</math>
<math name="intfeval">($a[1] /$a[2] )*$logf </math>
<math name="intfeval_C">$intfeval +C </math>
<math name="intfeval_no_abs">($a[1] /$a[2] )*ln($b +$a[2] *t) </math>
<math name="intfeval_no_abs_C">$intfeval_no_abs +C </math>

  
<abs name="divisor"><gcd>$a[1] $b $a[2]</gcd></abs>
<math name="logfalt">log(abs($b /$divisor +$a[2] /$divisor *t))</math>
<math name="intfevalalt">($a[1] /$a[2] )*$logfalt </math>
<math name="intfevalalt_C">$intfevalalt +C </math>
<math name="intfevalalt_no_abs">($a[1] /$a[2] )*log($b /$divisor +$a[2] /$divisor *t) </math>
<math name="intfevalalt_no_abs_C">$intfevalalt_no_abs +C </math>

<abs name="divisor2"><gcd>$b $a[2]</gcd></abs>
<math name="logfalt2">log(abs($b /$divisor2 +$a[2] /$divisor2 *t))</math>
<math name="intfevalalt2">($a[1] /$a[2] )*$logfalt2 </math>
<math name="intfevalalt2_C">$intfevalalt2 +C </math>
<math name="intfevalalt2_no_abs">($a[1] /$a[2] )*log($b /$divisor2 +$a[2] /$divisor2 *t)</math>
<math name="intfevalalt2_no_abs_C">$intfevalalt2_no_abs +C </math>

</setup>


Evaluate the following integral: <m>\displaystyle \int $f \, dt =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$intfeval_C </award>
<award>$intfevalalt_C </award>
<award>$intfevalalt2_C </award>
<award credit="0.67" name="noC">$intfeval </award>
<award credit="0.67" name="noCAlt">$intfevalalt </award>
<award credit="0.67" name="noCAlt2">$intfevalalt2 </award>
<award credit="0.5" name="noAbs">$intfeval_no_abs_C </award>
<award credit="0.5" name="noAbsAlt">$intfevalalt_no_abs_C </award>
<award credit="0.5" name="noAbsAlt2">$intfevalalt2_no_abs_C </award>
<award credit="0.33" name="noCAbs">$intfeval_no_abs </award>
<award credit="0.33" name="noCAbsAlt">$intfevalalt_no_abs </award>
<award credit="0.33" name="noCAbsAlt2">$intfevalalt2_no_abs </award>
</answer>
<feedback condition="$noC or $noCAlt or $noCAlt2">Don't forget the constant.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$noAbs or $noAbsAlt or $noAbsAlt2">Don't forget the absolute value.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$noCAbs or $noCAbsAlt or $noCAbsAlt2">Don't forget the absolute value and the constant.</feedback>



<solution>
<p>We first use the constant product rule for integrals to simplify:
</p>

<p>

<m>\displaystyle \int $f \, dt  = $a[1] \int $fpart \, dt</m>
</p>

<p>
Recall that the integral of one over a linear function of <m>t</m> is the natural logarithm of the absolute value of the linear function, divided by the coefficient of <m>t</m>:
</p>

<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int $fpart \, dt  = \frac{ $logf  }{ $a[2]  }+C</m>
</p>

<p>
Multiplying by <m>$a[1] </m> gives us our final solution:
</p>
  
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int $f \, dt  = $intfeval +C</m>
</p>

</solution>


</problem>